  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "New methods explicitly model noise, transcriptional bursting, raw-molecule spatial structure and cohort-level disease programs in single-cell data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is explicit generative or constrained modeling to separate biological variation from technical artifacts. stochprofIC uses distributional regression to correct noise and batch effects when inferring state variability from mini-bulk and split-pool data. PhysInfer fits burst size and frequency from static scRNA-seq with an unsupervised, physics-constrained network. scDeepSim adds a structured VAE with latent diffusion that allows controlled intervention on biological and technical factors for benchmarking. In spatial omics, spSheaf infers domains from raw transcript molecules without segmentation, outperforming SSAM and Points2Regions. SCOUBI maps axon-dendrite interfaces from extrasomatic RNA. SPIN integrates spatially informed co-expression networks with Bayesian regression for clinical prediction. A secondary cohort-scale thread links cell programs to phenotype. TriSCOPE spans ~4.2M nuclei from 627 Alzheimer's donors, and scCody uses generative optimal transport to separate aging-aligned from divergent disease remodeling. Brain-centric representation work includes SEMPER and the fMRI-based IC-GNN.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
